CI/CD Pipeline - Basic Documentation Notes

Azure DevOps + shared Enterprise Pipeline templates + JFrog/Lucius + Google Cloud Run
Draft reconstructed from the supplied pipeline screenshots and operational notes
Scope note: These are basic working notes, not a byte-for-byte transcription of the YAML. Values and names were read from screenshots and should be verified in the repository before production use. Secret values are intentionally not reproduced.

 
1. Quick Overview
• The repository contains a thin CI/CD entry-point file (azure-pipelines.yaml) and a larger CD implementation file (azure-pipelines-cd.yaml).
• CI build logic is largely delegated to a shared Enterprise Pipeline repository owned by the EPI team. Application teams consume tagged templates rather than maintaining the full CI implementation locally.
• CD logic is maintained in the application repository and deploys container images to Google Cloud Run for DEV, UAT, and PROD.
• The pipeline supports two paths: build a new image, or reuse an existing image version and perform deployment only.
• JFrog/Lucius is used as the source container registry. Google Artifact Registry (GAR) is the deployment-side registry. Podman is used to pull/tag/push images.
• Azure DevOps Secure Files stores JSON/service-account keys and the Lucius/JFrog authentication file used by the pipeline.
High-level execution flow
Manual pipeline run
 -> Select parameters (build/deploy environments/image version)
 -> If runBuild=true: shared CI template builds artifact/image
 -> If runBuild=false: validate the supplied existing image version
 -> Resolve enterprise image tag
 -> Authenticate to source registry and Google Cloud
 -> Transfer image: Lucius/JFrog -> Google Artifact Registry
 -> Deploy Cloud Run candidate revision
 -> Verify candidate revision becomes Ready
 -> Promote traffic where configured (UAT/PROD)
