5. Image Transfer and Cloud Run Deployment - Common Pattern
1. Resolve the image tag from the manual `imageVersion` parameter or from Azure DevOps build tags.
2. Download required Secure Files (GCP JSON key(s) and Lucius/JFrog authentication file).
3. Authenticate to Google Cloud using `gcloud auth activate-service-account` and set the target project.
4. Configure Docker/GAR authentication using `gcloud auth configure-docker` or an access-token-based Podman login.
5. Log in to Lucius/JFrog with the temporary auth file, then pull the source container image.
6. Retag the image for the target GAR repository and push it.
7. Set an Azure DevOps pipeline variable containing the deployed image URI.
8. Authenticate with the Cloud Run deployment/admin service account when a separate identity is required.
9. Deploy a Cloud Run candidate revision with no production traffic.
10. Compare latest-created and latest-ready revision names. Fail the job if the candidate did not become Ready.
11. Promote the Ready revision to traffic when the environment flow requires it.
6. Azure DevOps UI - Basic Operational Notes
• Under Pipelines > Pipelines, use Recent/All/Runs to find a pipeline and inspect recent executions. Open a run to see the stages, jobs, tasks, logs, parameters, and failure point.
• Under Pipelines > Environments, manage deployment environments such as DEV/UAT/PROD and their checks/approvals.
• Under Pipelines > Library, manage Secure Files and variable groups. JSON keys used by the YAML should be stored here as Secure Files, not committed to the repository.
• Other visible pipeline administration areas include Releases, Task groups, Deployment groups, and Build Tags. Build Tags are especially relevant because this CD YAML reads `version` and `short_commit_id` tags from the current build.
How to add an approver between environments
1. Go to Pipelines > Environments and select the target environment (for example UAT or PROD).
2. Open the environment checks/approvals configuration and choose Add check > Approvals.
3. Add the required user(s) or group(s), then configure instructions, timeout, and approval behavior according to team policy.
4. Save the check. A deployment job that targets that Azure DevOps environment will wait for the approval before the protected deployment proceeds.
Secure Files / JSON keys
1. Go to Pipelines > Library > Secure files.
2. Upload the approved JSON key/config file and grant only the pipelines/users that require access.
3. Reference the file in YAML through `DownloadSecureFile@1`. Give the task a name such as `gcpKey` or `gcpCloudRunAdminKey`.
4. Use the downloaded file through `$(<taskName>.secureFilePath)`; the pipeline should not hard-code the key contents.
5. When rotating a key, upload/replace the Secure File and confirm pipeline authorization before the next deployment.
